{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TWyixeqDcK3i3R6DULdhnW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Introduction}\n2\t\\label{sec:introduction}\n3\t\n4\tLLM agents are increasingly deployed for complex, long-horizon software engineering tasks---resolving GitHub issues, debugging production systems, and iterating on multi-file codebases across dozens of editing rounds \\citep{jimenez2024swebench,yang2024sweagent,wang2025openhands}.\n5\tAs these deployments scale, a critical failure mode has emerged that is qualitatively different from crashes or syntax errors: the agent \\emph{stagnates}---continuing to produce actions that appear purposeful while making no meaningful progress toward the task goal.\n6\t\\citet{orlanski2026slopcodebench} document this degradation on SlopCodeBench, recording verbosity increases of 77\\% and quality drops of 75.5\\% in long-horizon coding trajectories, with no agent fully solving any benchmark problem end-to-end.\n7\tCrucially, the dominant failure pattern is not simple repetition but what we term \\emph{semantic stagnation}: the agent issues varied commands, edits different files, and generates plausible-looking outputs, yet its behavior is functionally stuck.\n8\tThis ``looks busy but going in circles'' pattern defeats existing monitoring tools, because surface-level action diversity masks the underlying lack of progress.\n9\tWe observe this phenomenon at scale: across trajectories from two architecturally distinct agents---SWE-Agent \\citep{yang2024sweagent} and OpenHands \\citep{wang2025openhands}---on SWE-bench tasks \\citep{jimenez2024swebench}, 28.5\\% and 20.9\\% of sliding windows exhibit stagnation under our silver labeling scheme, respectively, confirming that the failure mode is both prevalent and highly agent-dependent.\n10\t\n11\tExisting approaches address aspects of this problem but fall short in three ways.\n12\tFirst, \\emph{repetition detectors} such as cycle detection \\citep{george2025cycledetection} and SpecRA \\citep{pipis2025reasoningloop} identify structural repetition---exact or near-exact loops in action sequences---but by design miss semantically diverse stagnation where the agent varies its surface behavior while remaining functionally stuck.\n13\tSecond, \\emph{step-level scoring} via process reward models \\citep{xi2025agentprm} evaluates individual actions in isolation, discarding the window-level temporal dynamics that distinguish transient setbacks from sustained stagnation---a distinction that requires reasoning over sequences of consecutive actions \\citep{chen2025monitoring}.\n14\tA sequence of failed commands followed by a strategy switch, for instance, carries different diagnostic information than the same commands evaluated independently.\n15\tThird, \\emph{LLM self-evaluation} \\citep{shinn2023reflexion} is both expensive and fundamentally limited by the stuck model's own judgment; our LLM-as-judge baseline achieves only 0.663 macro-F1 at \\$11.66 per test set, well below lightweight feature-based alternatives.\n16\tMore fundamentally, none of these approaches asks the prior question: \\emph{what signals actually carry stagnation information?}\n17\tNo systematic study has investigated whether detecting subtle agent failure requires sophisticated semantic understanding or whether simpler structural patterns suffice.\n18\t\n19\tWe address this question through a systematic empirical study (Figure~\\ref{fig:pipeline}).\n20\tUsing a weak supervision framework \\citep{ratner2016dataprogramming,ratner2017snorkel}, we construct silver-labeled datasets from over 205K windows spanning SWE-Agent (54,809 windows, 28.5\\% silver-labeled stagnation) and OpenHands (150,652 windows, 20.9\\% stagnation).\n21\tThree labeling functions provide complementary noisy votes: command repetition ($\\lambda_1$) targets repetitive behavior, file diff delta ($\\lambda_2$) targets output productivity, and outcome backpropagation ($\\lambda_3$) propagates task-level outcomes backward through the trajectory.\n22\tMajority vote aggregation produces window-level binary labels with confidence scores.\n23\tOur window-level formulation ($k{=}5$ consecutive actions, stride 1) captures short-range temporal patterns---such as repeated failures followed by strategy changes---that step-level approaches cannot express, while providing the granularity to pinpoint \\emph{when} stagnation begins and ends within a trajectory, enabling targeted interventions.\n24\tAgainst these silver labels, we systematically compare hand-crafted structural features, a neural embedding probe, and an LLM-as-judge baseline.\n25\t\n26\tOur study yields three principal findings.\n27\t\n28\t\\textbf{Finding 1: Structural features enable effective zero-data cross-coding-agent transfer, but probes surpass them with fine-tuning.}\n29\tThirty hand-crafted features achieve 0.995 macro-F1 in-domain and 0.967 cross-coding-agent (SWE-Agent $\\to$ OpenHands) with zero target data, outperforming the GRU probe \\citep{cho2014gru} by 36.9\\,pp and the best LLM judge by 16.8\\,pp.\n30\tWith target fine-tuning the probe reaches 0.976, surpassing the structural baseline on all windows (0.967).\n31\tThe in-domain score reflects circular evaluation (\\S\\ref{sec:circular}); the cross-coding-agent score breaks this circularity, as models trained on one agent's LF distribution are tested on another with substantially different firing patterns.\n32\tA feature overlap ablation (\\S\\ref{sec:feature_overlap}) confirms that 11 LF-overlapping features alone achieve 0.977 cross-coding-agent F1 on all windows, while 19 residual features collapse to 0.566---the features that generalize encode universal stagnation signals, not LF artifacts.\n33\tThis suggests that lightweight structural monitoring (no GPU, ${<}$5\\,ms per window) may be more practical than neural or LLM-based approaches for real-time agent oversight.\n34\t\n35\t\\textbf{Finding 2: Label quality, not LF selection, is the dominant cross-coding-agent transfer bottleneck.}\n36\tLabeling functions exhibit systematic temporal dynamics that reverse across agents: command repetition ($\\lambda_1$) leads stagnation onset on SWE-Agent by $-1.87$ steps but lags on OpenHands by $+3.32$ steps.\n37\tA label quality audit reveals that fixing OH label version bias---correcting stagnation rate from 6.56\\% to 21.4\\%---improves 5\\% fine-tuning F1 from 0.570 (under previous label version) to 0.850.\n38\tPrevalence-matched ablation shows that $\\lambda_1$'s core value is coverage ($-29.4$\\,pp when removed, $p \\approx 0$), not label precision: it fires on only 5.39\\% of OH windows with precision 97.91\\% (FP rate 2.3\\%).\n39\t\n40\t\\textbf{Finding 3: No label model improves over majority vote.}\n41\tFive label models---three temporal (TWV, HMM \\citep{safranchik2020linkedhmm}, lag-aware HMM) and two generative (FlyingSquid \\citep{fu2020flyingsquid}, Snorkel MeTaL \\citep{ratner2019snorkelmetal})---all fail to surpass majority vote on downstream detection.\n42\tTWV achieves marginally higher self-evaluated F1 but degrades downstream probe performance by 2.57\\,pp; Snorkel MeTaL collapses catastrophically ($-$63.6\\,pp).\n43\tThe bottleneck is LF quantity, not aggregation sophistication: three binary LFs produce too little inter-LF conflict for any method to exploit.\n44\t\n45\tOur contributions are:\n46\t\\begin{enumerate}\n47\t    \\item \\textbf{Weak supervision methodology for agent monitoring.} We design a silver-labeling pipeline (3 LFs + majority vote) over 205K+ windows from two coding agents, enabling systematic comparison of detection methods without manual annotation.\n48\t    \\item \\textbf{Label quality as transfer bottleneck.} We show that labeling functions exhibit agent-specific temporal reversals and that fixing label version bias (stag rate 6.56\\%$\\to$21.4\\% on the embedding subset) is the dominant factor for cross-coding-agent transfer, informing future weak-supervision design on sequential agent data.\n49\t    \\item \\textbf{Label model negative finding.} We show that five label models---three temporal and two generative---fail to improve over majority vote for downstream detection. The bottleneck is LF quantity, not aggregation method: the 3-LF regime produces insufficient inter-LF conflict for any approach to add value.\n50\t    \\item \\textbf{Deployment-regime trade-off.} In zero-data settings, 30 structural features outperform the neural probe by 36.9\\,pp and the best LLM judge by 16.8\\,pp on silver labels (no GPU, ${<}$5\\,ms per window). With target-agent fine-tuning, the probe surpasses structural features on all windows (0.976 vs.\\ 0.967 macro-F1), delineating when structural monitoring suffices and when fine-tuning is warranted.\n51\t\\end{enumerate}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01A9kMh3ZhLJdi8fxaMkWqoB","session_id":"5d5323cd-d040-44cc-ba70-9f4e536cb20e","uuid":"d2c8137a-fafb-4714-a1bf-056f1a3ecd9d","timestamp":"2026-05-15T15:37:22.616Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Xxua7N3mzVVb94yJeRsg3s","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014oFUa9LSFk3DNxGozQdbDu","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/agent_progress_probe/docs/paper/sections/related_work.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11832,"cache_read_input_tokens":16387,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11832,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":36,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01A9kMh3ZhLJdi8fxaMkWqoB","session_id":"5d5323cd-d040-44cc-ba70-9f4e536cb20e","uuid":"623e44cb-0859-43f5-9090-4033d1a53d7b"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014oFUa9LSFk3DNxGozQdbDu","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Related Work}\n2\t\\label{sec:related_work}\n3\t\n4\t\\paragraph{Process Reward Models.}\n5\tPRMs assign step-level quality scores to guide agent behavior \\citep{xi2025agentprm,choudhury2025prm,fan2026agentrm}, with benchmarks revealing degradation on long-horizon trajectories \\citep{wang2026planrewardbench} and the advantage of specialized over general-purpose PRMs \\citep{li2026toolprmbench}.\n6\t\\citet{le2022coderl} take a complementary value-function approach operating on program states.\n7\tOur work differs in operating at window level without outcome labels, targeting real-time monitoring ($<$5\\,ms) rather than training signal generation.\n8\t\n9\t\\paragraph{Agent Self-Reflection.}\n10\tSelf-reflection methods \\citep{shinn2023reflexion,yao2023tot,zhou2024lats} prompt agents to diagnose their own failures, but LLMs cannot reliably self-correct without external feedback \\citep{huang2024selfcorrect,kamoi2024selfcorrection}.\n11\tWe address the prior question---\\emph{when} is the agent stuck---using external structural signals.\n12\t\n13\t\\paragraph{Weak Supervision for Sequential Data.}\n14\tData programming \\citep{ratner2016dataprogramming,ratner2017snorkel} enables dataset creation through labeling functions, extended to sequential data via linked HMMs \\citep{safranchik2020linkedhmm}, multi-resolution LFs \\citep{sala2019multiresolution}, and heterogeneous aggregation \\citep{lison2021skweak}.\n15\tWe apply this paradigm to agent trajectories, finding---unlike \\citeauthor{safranchik2020linkedhmm}'s NER results---that temporal label models do not improve over majority vote in our low-conflict 3-LF regime (\\S\\ref{sec:temporal_label_models}).\n16\t\n17\t\\paragraph{Noisy Labels and Label Leakage.}\n18\tOur silver labels connect to noisy label learning \\citep{han2018coteaching,li2020dividemix}, though our noise is temporally block-correlated rather than instance-level.\n19\tThe structural feature--label overlap instantiates label leakage \\citep{kaufman2012leakage} and Goodhart's Law \\citep{manheim2018goodhart}; we mitigate this through cross-coding-agent evaluation and a human-annotated gold set (\\S\\ref{sec:gold_set}).\n20\t\n21\t\\paragraph{Agent Monitoring and Degradation.}\n22\t\\citet{orlanski2026slopcodebench} document coding agent degradation; \\citet{chen2025monitoring} establish that sequence-level context is necessary for monitoring; \\citet{kale2025weaktostrong} show weak monitors can oversee stronger agents; and \\citet{ma2024agentboard} introduce fine-grained progress tracking.\n23\t\\citet{cemri2025multiagentfail} and \\citet{zhu2025agentfail} catalog systematic failure modes including cascading failures in long-horizon tasks.\n24\tWe complement these with a systematic comparison demonstrating that lightweight structural features suffice for detection and transfer.\n25\t\n26\t\\paragraph{Cycle Detection and Process Mining.}\n27\tCycle detectors \\citep{george2025cycledetection,pipis2025reasoningloop} target structural repetition but miss semantically diverse stagnation; \\citet{liu2026trajad} train trajectory verifiers for broader anomaly detection.\n28\tOur structural features draw on process mining \\citep{vanderaalst2016processmining}, and the success of hand-crafted features in cross-coding-agent transfer parallels classical findings where feature-based methods outperform learned representations for narrow detection tasks \\citep{liu2008isolationforest}.\n29\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01A9kMh3ZhLJdi8fxaMkWqoB","session_id":"5d5323cd-d040-44cc-ba70-9f4e536cb20e","uuid":"958f123e-4dcb-48be-b125-b160557e38d8","timestamp":"2026-05-15T15:37:29.734Z"}
