{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018ksKZdpf2DRA5RSyL5vqRn","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## R2 Roundtable Response\n\n**Points of Agreement with R1:**\n\nR1's W1 (circularity) is the most important finding in either review and I fully agree. The 0.977 cross-agent AUC from LF-overlapping features versus 0.566 from residual features is devastating -- it means the probe is largely reconstructing its own supervision signal, not detecting an independent construct called \"stagnation.\" This aligns with my original novelty concern: the contribution reduces to showing that ML classifiers can approximate their own training labels.\n\nI also agree on W2 (gold set quality). Kappa 0.39 is \"fair\" agreement at best, which puts a low ceiling on any claim about the probe capturing a real phenomenon. W5 (only two agents, same domain) matches my related work concern about generalizability. W4 (incomplete deployment analysis with mock data) is new to me and concerning -- if confirmed, it undermines the practical framing that was one of the paper's stronger aspects.\n\n**Points of Disagreement with R1:**\n\nR1 is somewhat generous in listing \"comprehensive ablation design\" as a strength. The ablations are comprehensive in quantity but the feature overlap ablation actually undermines the paper's thesis rather than supporting it. A well-designed ablation study should have caught this circularity issue during development, not surfaced it as a post-hoc concern.\n\nI think R1 underweights the related work gap. The paper fails to engage with process mining and workflow analytics literature that has studied similar trajectory-based productivity signals for over a decade. This is not a minor omission -- it affects whether the framing as \"novel\" is justified.\n\n**Score Adjustment:**\n\nI maintain my 3/6. R1's circularity analysis (W1) provides concrete quantitative evidence for concerns I raised qualitatively about novelty. If anything, the evidence pushes toward a lower score, but the honest disclosure R1 notes and the useful negative results on TLMs prevent me from going to 2.\n\n**Key Issues for Consolidated Review:**\n\n1. **Circularity is fatal in current form.** The probe's cross-agent \"transfer\" is dominated by LF-reconstructive features. The paper needs residual-feature-only evaluation as the primary result, not an ablation footnote.\n2. **Gold standard is inadequate.** Kappa 0.39 with N=52 for IAA cannot anchor claims about ground truth.\n3. **Generalizability is undemonstrated.** Two agents, one domain, one benchmark.\n4. **Deployment claims need honest scoping.** 2.7% coverage on the second agent is not deployable; mock data in analysis scripts (if confirmed) should be flagged.\n5. **Missing engagement with process mining literature.**"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a863e34969ed8b56d (use SendMessage with to: 'a863e34969ed8b56d' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 16123\ntool_uses: 0\nduration_ms: 19795</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5d5323cd-d040-44cc-ba70-9f4e536cb20e","uuid":"25b70bf4-e8bc-4145-8787-5f5bc97ded06","timestamp":"2026-05-15T15:41:15.971Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) in a roundtable discussion. You previously reviewed this paper focusing on novelty and related work, and gave it a 3/6. Now you have received Reviewer 1's review (focused on methods and experiments). Read R1's review below and provide:\n\n1. Points of agreement with R1\n2. Points of disagreement with R1\n3. Any adjustment to your score (with justification)\n4. Key issues that should appear in the final consolidated review\n\nKeep your response concise (under 400 words).\n\n## R1's Review Summary\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject), Soundness 2/4**\n\n**Key Strengths R1 identified:**\n- Honest circularity disclosure\n- Comprehensive ablation design\n- Statistical reporting above average (paired bootstrap, McNemar, instance isolation)\n- Useful negative finding on TLMs\n- Practical framing (cost, latency)\n- LF temporal dynamics analysis (cmd_rep reversal across agents)\n\n**Key Weaknesses R1 identified:**\n- W1: Circularity more severe than acknowledged — LF-overlapping features alone get 0.977 cross-agent; residual features collapse to 0.566. Cross-agent \"transfer\" is largely LF reconstruction on new agent.\n- W2: Gold set underpowered — kappa=0.39, N=52 for 3-annotator IAA, N=505 total. Probe not evaluated on full gold set including OH.\n- W3: Matched-scope comparison conflates evaluation conditions — headline +37.2pp compares different N values (150K vs 6K).\n- W4: Deployment analysis experiments appear incomplete — compute_waste_audit.json shows OH data=0; pareto_curve.py has generate_mock_data() function.\n- W5: Only two agents from same domain on same benchmark.\n- W6: 2-LF \"deployable\" config has 2.7% coverage on OH — not deployable.\n- W7: No online/intervention evaluation.\n\n**R1's Missing Experiments:**\n- Gold-set probe evaluation on OH windows\n- Larger gold set with higher IAA\n- Third agent or non-SE domain\n- Calibration analysis\n- Window stride/overlap sensitivity\n- Cost-benefit analysis of full pipeline vs direct annotation\n\n**R1's Critical Questions:**\n- Feature overlap ablation shows LF-overlapping features alone exceed full model cross-agent. How is this \"detecting true stagnation\"?\n- Human ceiling given kappa=0.39?\n- Why no probe on full gold set?\n- Nature of the \"version mismatch\" bug?\n- What happens to 97.3% of OH windows where 2-LF abstains?\n","agentId":"a863e34969ed8b56d","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## R2 Roundtable Response\n\n**Points of Agreement with R1:**\n\nR1's W1 (circularity) is the most important finding in either review and I fully agree. The 0.977 cross-agent AUC from LF-overlapping features versus 0.566 from residual features is devastating -- it means the probe is largely reconstructing its own supervision signal, not detecting an independent construct called \"stagnation.\" This aligns with my original novelty concern: the contribution reduces to showing that ML classifiers can approximate their own training labels.\n\nI also agree on W2 (gold set quality). Kappa 0.39 is \"fair\" agreement at best, which puts a low ceiling on any claim about the probe capturing a real phenomenon. W5 (only two agents, same domain) matches my related work concern about generalizability. W4 (incomplete deployment analysis with mock data) is new to me and concerning -- if confirmed, it undermines the practical framing that was one of the paper's stronger aspects.\n\n**Points of Disagreement with R1:**\n\nR1 is somewhat generous in listing \"comprehensive ablation design\" as a strength. The ablations are comprehensive in quantity but the feature overlap ablation actually undermines the paper's thesis rather than supporting it. A well-designed ablation study should have caught this circularity issue during development, not surfaced it as a post-hoc concern.\n\nI think R1 underweights the related work gap. The paper fails to engage with process mining and workflow analytics literature that has studied similar trajectory-based productivity signals for over a decade. This is not a minor omission -- it affects whether the framing as \"novel\" is justified.\n\n**Score Adjustment:**\n\nI maintain my 3/6. R1's circularity analysis (W1) provides concrete quantitative evidence for concerns I raised qualitatively about novelty. If anything, the evidence pushes toward a lower score, but the honest disclosure R1 notes and the useful negative results on TLMs prevent me from going to 2.\n\n**Key Issues for Consolidated Review:**\n\n1. **Circularity is fatal in current form.** The probe's cross-agent \"transfer\" is dominated by LF-reconstructive features. The paper needs residual-feature-only evaluation as the primary result, not an ablation footnote.\n2. **Gold standard is inadequate.** Kappa 0.39 with N=52 for IAA cannot anchor claims about ground truth.\n3. **Generalizability is undemonstrated.** Two agents, one domain, one benchmark.\n4. **Deployment claims need honest scoping.** 2.7% coverage on the second agent is not deployable; mock data in analysis scripts (if confirmed) should be flagged.\n5. **Missing engagement with process mining literature.**"}],"totalDurationMs":19795,"totalTokens":16123,"totalToolUseCount":0,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6449,"cache_read_input_tokens":9038,"output_tokens":634,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6449},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":634,"cache_read_input_tokens":9038,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6449,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6449,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a2fd47d85944f3e4f","tool_use_id":"toolu_01PFrPzC2PTYrQEk8n8NEW2c","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1 cross-review of R2 findings","usage":{"total_tokens":28341,"tool_uses":5,"duration_ms":44274},"uuid":"73be1892-b6df-4179-ba3d-8e47127402c6","session_id":"5d5323cd-d040-44cc-ba70-9f4e536cb20e"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PFrPzC2PTYrQEk8n8NEW2c","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Here is my R1 response to R2's review:\n\n---\n\n**R1 Response to R2's Review**\n\n**1. Points of Agreement**\n\nI agree with R2 on the most substantive criticisms:\n\n- **W1 (Limited novelty):** This was central to my own assessment. The contribution is an engineering evaluation, not a methodological advance. Every component is off-the-shelf.\n- **W2 (Tautological headline):** R2's observation that 11 LF-overlapping features alone hit 0.977 cross-agent -- exceeding the full 30-feature model -- is the single most damaging finding in the paper. The authors acknowledge circularity but understate how thoroughly LF-overlapping features explain the result. The residual features collapsing to 0.566 cross-agent means the \"beyond LF reconstruction\" claim rests almost entirely on the gold set (N=505, kappa=0.39), which R2 rightly calls underwhelming.\n- **W4 (Two agents, one benchmark):** Both agents operate on SWE-bench. Cross-agent is not cross-domain. I flagged this same limitation.\n- **W5 (Gold set):** Kappa=0.39 is \"fair\" agreement at best. With N=505, confidence intervals on the 4.2pp GB-over-MV advantage are wide. This is the paper's primary non-circular evidence, and it is thin.\n\n**2. Points of Disagreement**\n\n- **W3 (Related work):** R2's missing-citations list is partly unfair. The paper does cite process mining (van der Aalst), cycle detection, and agent monitoring work. NeMo Guardrails and AgentTrek operate at a different abstraction level (policy enforcement and trajectory generation, respectively). The probing literature (Belinkov, Hewitt & Manning) is tangential -- this paper's \"probe\" is just a GRU classifier, not a probe in the interpretability sense. I would not penalize heavily here.\n- **W6 (Missing deployment validation):** While desirable, no online evaluation is standard for this type of methods paper. This is a limitation, not a weakness warranting score reduction.\n\n**3. Score Adjustment**\n\nI maintain my **Weak Reject (3/6)**. R2's feature-overlap analysis (W2) reinforces my concern that the paper's core claims are weaker than presented. However, R2's related work complaints are somewhat overstated. Net: no change.\n\n**4. Key Issues for Consolidated Review**\n\n1. **The 11-vs-30 feature result undermines the central claim.** If LF-overlapping features alone exceed the full model cross-agent, the paper demonstrates LF design quality, not a detection method.\n2. **Gold set is insufficient as the sole non-circular evidence.** N=505 with kappa=0.39 cannot bear the evidential weight the paper assigns it.\n3. **Single benchmark (SWE-bench), two agents.** Cross-domain generalization is untested.\n4. **No methodological novelty.** The contribution is empirical, and the empirical story is circular in its strongest results.\n5. **Temporal label model negative result is genuinely useful** and should be preserved in any revision -- this is the paper's most transferable finding."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a2fd47d85944f3e4f (use SendMessage with to: 'a2fd47d85944f3e4f' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 29100\ntool_uses: 5\nduration_ms: 44276</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5d5323cd-d040-44cc-ba70-9f4e536cb20e","uuid":"adbc6477-0821-43e9-b051-69dbc818d52e","timestamp":"2026-05-15T15:41:25.302Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) in a roundtable discussion. You previously reviewed this paper focusing on methods and experiments, and gave it a Weak Reject (3/6). Now you have received Reviewer 2's review (focused on novelty and related work). Read R2's review below and provide:\n\n1. Points of agreement with R2\n2. Points of disagreement with R2\n3. Any adjustment to your score (with justification)\n4. Key issues that should appear in the final consolidated review\n\nKeep your response concise (under 400 words).\n\n## R2's Review Summary\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n\n**Key Strengths R2 identified:**\n- Honest circularity treatment\n- Useful negative finding on temporal label models\n- Thorough ablation/diagnostics\n- Practical deployment framing\n- Scale of evaluation (205K+ windows)\n\n**Key Weaknesses R2 identified:**\n- W1: Limited novelty — all components (structural features + GB/RF, GRU probe, MV aggregation, sliding windows) are well-established. The paper is competent engineering evaluation, not a paradigm shift.\n- W2: Headline result partially tautological — 11 LF-overlapping features alone achieve 0.977 cross-agent (exceeding full model 0.967), residual features collapse to 0.566. Gold set (0.839, N=505, kappa=0.39) is modest.\n- W3: Related work sparse in key areas — missing runtime monitoring/guardrails, temporal anomaly detection, agent trajectory evaluation, probing literature, active learning for agent oversight, process mining.\n- W4: Only two agents on one benchmark — cross-agent but not cross-domain.\n- W5: Gold set validation underwhelming — kappa=0.39, N=505.\n- W6: Missing deployment validation — no online evaluation, no user study, all retrospective.\n\n**R2's Missing Related Work:**\n- NeMo Guardrails, GuardAgent\n- Agent trajectory evaluation (AgentTrek, tau-bench)\n- Probing literature (Belinkov, Hewitt & Manning)\n- Process mining for SE\n- Reward hacking / specification gaming\n\n**R2's Questions:**\n- 11 LF-overlapping features exceed full model cross-agent — how to reconcile with \"beyond LF reconstruction\" claim?\n- cmd_rep fires 65.5% SWE vs 5.4% OH — is cross-agent transfer just the remaining LFs driving signal on OH?\n- Gold set may be biased toward cases where structural features are informative (sampling stratified by confidence/LF agreement)\n","agentId":"a2fd47d85944f3e4f","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Here is my R1 response to R2's review:\n\n---\n\n**R1 Response to R2's Review**\n\n**1. Points of Agreement**\n\nI agree with R2 on the most substantive criticisms:\n\n- **W1 (Limited novelty):** This was central to my own assessment. The contribution is an engineering evaluation, not a methodological advance. Every component is off-the-shelf.\n- **W2 (Tautological headline):** R2's observation that 11 LF-overlapping features alone hit 0.977 cross-agent -- exceeding the full 30-feature model -- is the single most damaging finding in the paper. The authors acknowledge circularity but understate how thoroughly LF-overlapping features explain the result. The residual features collapsing to 0.566 cross-agent means the \"beyond LF reconstruction\" claim rests almost entirely on the gold set (N=505, kappa=0.39), which R2 rightly calls underwhelming.\n- **W4 (Two agents, one benchmark):** Both agents operate on SWE-bench. Cross-agent is not cross-domain. I flagged this same limitation.\n- **W5 (Gold set):** Kappa=0.39 is \"fair\" agreement at best. With N=505, confidence intervals on the 4.2pp GB-over-MV advantage are wide. This is the paper's primary non-circular evidence, and it is thin.\n\n**2. Points of Disagreement**\n\n- **W3 (Related work):** R2's missing-citations list is partly unfair. The paper does cite process mining (van der Aalst), cycle detection, and agent monitoring work. NeMo Guardrails and AgentTrek operate at a different abstraction level (policy enforcement and trajectory generation, respectively). The probing literature (Belinkov, Hewitt & Manning) is tangential -- this paper's \"probe\" is just a GRU classifier, not a probe in the interpretability sense. I would not penalize heavily here.\n- **W6 (Missing deployment validation):** While desirable, no online evaluation is standard for this type of methods paper. This is a limitation, not a weakness warranting score reduction.\n\n**3. Score Adjustment**\n\nI maintain my **Weak Reject (3/6)**. R2's feature-overlap analysis (W2) reinforces my concern that the paper's core claims are weaker than presented. However, R2's related work complaints are somewhat overstated. Net: no change.\n\n**4. Key Issues for Consolidated Review**\n\n1. **The 11-vs-30 feature result undermines the central claim.** If LF-overlapping features alone exceed the full model cross-agent, the paper demonstrates LF design quality, not a detection method.\n2. **Gold set is insufficient as the sole non-circular evidence.** N=505 with kappa=0.39 cannot bear the evidential weight the paper assigns it.\n3. **Single benchmark (SWE-bench), two agents.** Cross-domain generalization is untested.\n4. **No methodological novelty.** The contribution is empirical, and the empirical story is circular in its strongest results.\n5. **Temporal label model negative result is genuinely useful** and should be preserved in any revision -- this is the paper's most transferable finding."}],"totalDurationMs":44276,"totalTokens":29100,"totalToolUseCount":5,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3394,"cache_read_input_tokens":24934,"output_tokens":771,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3394},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":771,"cache_read_input_tokens":24934,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3394,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3394,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":5,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01U35Zwn7M3Z8Htf5gs1n9KH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the cross-review responses. Both reviewers maintain their 3/6 scores. Let me now synthesize the final consolidated review report (Step 3-4 of the roundtable).\n\nKey convergence points:\n1. Both agree on circularity as the central issue — LF-overlapping features alone achieve 0.977 cross-agent, exceeding the full model\n2. Both agree the gold set is underpowered (kappa=0.39, N=505)\n3. Both agree on limited generalizability (2 agents, 1 benchmark, same domain)\n\n\n4. Both see the TLM negative finding as the paper's strongest contribution\n5. Both land on 3/6 Weak Reject\n\nThe main tensions are around how much weight to give the related work gaps versus the ablation design strengths, and whether the missing process mining literature is a significant oversight. Now I'll draft the final consolidated report and submit it.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":735,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5001,"cache_read_input_tokens":62062,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5001},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5d5323cd-d040-44cc-ba70-9f4e536cb20e","uuid":"4f2a98d1-c719-4146-abbd-4a99c99e7db6"}
